# Network management and web interaction

The goal of this part is to receive the inputs from the players' phones and give them to the game.
Players open a web page on their phone and use it as a controller. The game runs on the IoT board and
is shown on the LED matrix. We use CrowCpp (already in `include/crow`) as the web server, and
WebSockets for the live inputs.

Status: this is the design. It is not implemented yet. Right now `Network` only shows a "Hello World"
page on `/`.

## What the repo asks for

- Use a `Network` object with CrowCpp (README, "What we need" number 2).
- Do not change the `Game` interface: `addPlayer`, `removePlayer`, `updatePlayer`.
- `MainMenu` is the only entry point for players. `[0, n]` votes for game n and `[1, ...]` is sent to
  the current game.
- The network must not know anything about Pong or the LED display.
- The board has only one CPU core, so the network part must stay light.

## How it works

```
 PHONE (browser)                  BOARD (1 CPU core)
+---------------+  HTTP          +--------------------------------------+
| web page      |--------------->| Crow threads                         |
| (HTML + JS)   |  WebSocket     |  - send the page                     |
|               |<==============>|  - check every message               |
+---------------+                |  - put the message in a queue        |
                                 +------------------+-------------------+
                                                    | queue
                                 +------------------v-------------------+
                                 | Main thread (game loop), each frame: |
                                 |  1. take messages from the queue     |
                                 |  2. update the game                  |
                                 |  3. draw and show on the LED matrix  |
                                 +--------------------------------------+
```

The phone loads the page once with HTTP. After that the page opens a WebSocket. A WebSocket stays
open, so the phone and the board can send messages to each other at any time. We use it instead of
normal HTTP requests because a controller sends many small updates every second, and one open
connection is much cheaper for a single-core board. The messages are small JSON texts. The phone sends
normalised numbers (for example 0 to 1000) and not pixel positions, so the size of the matrix does not
matter.

## Message flow

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant P as Phone
    participant C as Crow thread
    participant Q as Command queue
    participant M as Main loop
    participant G as MainMenu

    P->>C: HTTP GET /
    C-->>P: index.html and static files
    P->>C: WebSocket /ws, join with name
    C->>Q: Join command
    C-->>P: welcome with token
    M->>Q: dispatch drains the queue
    M->>G: addPlayer
    P->>C: input d=523
    C->>Q: Input command, latest value wins
    M->>Q: dispatch drains the queue
    M->>G: updatePlayer with 1 and 523
    M->>G: update and render
```

## Threads and the queue

Crow runs on its own threads, and the game loop runs on the main thread. `MainMenu` and the games are
not thread-safe, so the network threads must not call them directly. Two threads changing the same data
at the same time can break the game.

Our solution is a command queue:

- The network threads only check the message and put it in the queue.
- Once per frame the main thread empties the queue and calls `addPlayer`, `removePlayer` and
  `updatePlayer` itself.
- So only one thread touches the game, and the game classes do not need any change.
- We did not use a mutex, because it would be locked during the slow rendering and would block the
  network.

For the position input, only the newest value of each player is kept, because the phone sends more
updates than the board can draw. Join, leave, rename and vote are never dropped.

The network part owns the `Player` objects. A player is deleted only after the game has removed it, so
the game never uses a deleted pointer.

## Players and reconnecting

- When a player joins, the server creates a random token and uses it as the player ID. The phone saves
  it in `localStorage`. We do not use the IP address, because it can change or be shared.
- Phones close the connection when the screen locks. The server keeps the player for a short time
  (grace period). If the phone comes back with its token, it continues as the same player.
- If the grace period ends, `removePlayer` is called.
- The page sends a `ping` every 5 seconds. If the server hears nothing for too long, the connection is
  treated as closed.

## Messages

Phone to server:

| Message | Example | What happens |
|---|---|---|
| `join` | `{"t":"join","name":"Alice","token":"..."}` | New player, or the same player again if the token is known |
| `name` | `{"t":"name","name":"Bob"}` | Change the name |
| `vote` | `{"t":"vote","game":2}` | `updatePlayer( p, [0, 2] )` |
| `input` | `{"t":"input","d":[523]}` | `updatePlayer( p, [1, 523] )` |
| `ping` | `{"t":"ping"}` | Keep the connection alive |
| `leave` | `{"t":"leave"}` | `removePlayer( p )` |

Server to phone:

| Message | Example | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `welcome` | `{"t":"welcome","token":"...","name":"Alice"}` | Join accepted |
| `scene` | `{"t":"scene","scene":"game","game":0,"games":1}` | The active game changed |
| `pong` | `{"t":"pong"}` | Answer to `ping` |
| `error` | `{"t":"error","msg":"..."}` | The message was not valid |

`d` is a list of integers. The game decides what they mean. The network only checks that there are 1 to
8 numbers and adds the command code `1` in front.

## Web page

The page is in `www/templates/index.html` and `www/static/`. It uses plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript,
with no libraries and nothing from the internet, because the board may not have internet.

- Join screen: the player types a name.
- Lobby screen: the player votes for a game.
- Controller screen: a big touch slider. The page sends the value only when it changes, about 30
  times per second at most.
- If the connection is lost, the page shows a message and reconnects automatically with the saved token.

Touch works over normal HTTP. Phone tilt sensors need HTTPS, so we do not use them.

## Safety checks

Anyone on the network can send messages to the server, so every message is checked before it reaches
the game. These values are proposals:

- Maximum message size: 256 bytes, text only.
- Maximum about 60 messages per second for each connection.
- `d`: 1 to 8 integers, each between -1,000,000 and 1,000,000.
- Name: maximum 16 characters, control characters removed.
- `vote`: must be a valid game number.
- Maximum number of players: `max_players`.

## Config file

New settings are optional. If a key is missing, the program prints a warning and uses the default.

| Key | Default | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `port` | `8080` | Server port (already exists) |
| `max_players` | `32` | Maximum players |
| `reconnect_grace_s` | `15` | Seconds a disconnected player is kept |
| `heartbeat_timeout_s` | `20` | Seconds without a message before the connection is closed |

## Closing the program

The README says the program crashes when it is closed. The reason is that `main()` ends while the
server thread is still running, and C++ then calls `std::terminate`. The fix:

1. The main loop ends (window closed or Ctrl+C).
2. `Network::stop()` closes the connections and calls Crow's `stop()`.
3. `main()` calls `join()` on the server thread.

## Problems in the current code

- The server thread is never joined, which causes the crash above.
- In `MainMenu::update()`, if the winning vote goes back to the menu (`-1`) while players are still
  there (for example a voter disconnects), the code uses `_games[-1]`, which is out of bounds.
- In `MainMenu::updatePlayer()`, a `break` is missing after the vote case, so a wrong vote number is
  sent to the game.
- The `games` list in `main.cpp` is empty, so there is nothing to vote for yet.

